CAT = "auwahPlatform"; SEG = "PLT"
Q = [
('How high above the working surface should the top rail of a guardrail be?',
 '900 to 1100 mm', ['600 to 800 mm', '750 to 900 mm', '1200 to 1500 mm'],
 'The code of practice recommends a top rail between 900 mm and 1100 mm above the working surface, with a mid-rail and a toe-board.'),
('Besides a top rail, what should a guardrail system include?',
 'A mid-rail and a toe-board', ['A gate and an access ladder', 'A harness and a lanyard', 'A net and a warning sign'],
 'A guardrail should have a top rail, a mid-rail and a toe-board or mesh infill so people cannot slip under it and objects cannot fall off the edge.'),
('What is the main purpose of a toe-board on a work platform?',
 'To stop things falling off', ['To stop the platform tipping', 'To raise the guardrail height', 'To give workers a footrest'],
 'Toe-boards along open edges stop tools, materials and debris being kicked off the platform onto people below.'),
('A light duty scaffold is rated for up to what load per bay?',
 '225 kg', ['120 kg', '450 kg', '675 kg'],
 'Light duty platforms take up to 225 kg per bay, including people, tools, materials and debris. That is about one 80 kg worker plus 145 kg of gear.'),
('A medium duty scaffold is rated for up to what load per bay?',
 '450 kg', ['225 kg', '675 kg', '900 kg'],
 'Medium duty platforms take up to 450 kg per bay and suit general trades work. They should be at least four planks (about 900 mm) wide.'),
('Bricklaying and concreting from a scaffold need which duty rating?',
 'Heavy duty', ['Light duty', 'Medium duty', 'Any duty rating'],
 'Heavy duty platforms take up to 675 kg per bay and are needed for bricklaying, concreting, demolition and other heavy loads or impact.'),
('How wide should a light duty scaffold platform be?',
 'Two planks wide (about 450 mm)', ['One plank wide (about 225 mm)', 'Four planks wide (about 900 mm)', 'Five planks wide (about 1000 mm)'],
 'Light duty platforms should be at least two planks wide, about 450 mm. Medium duty needs four planks and heavy duty five.'),
('How often must a scaffold and its supporting structure be inspected by a competent person while in use?',
 'At least every 30 days', ['At least every 90 days', 'Only when first built', 'At least once a year'],
 'Scaffolds must be inspected at least every 30 days, and also before use resumes after an incident such as a storm or impact, and after repairs.'),
('A scaffold from which a person could fall more than 4 metres has just been built. What is needed before it is used?',
 'Written sign-off by a competent person', ['A photo of it sent to the client', 'A verbal OK from the site labourer', 'A sign at the base saying it is finished'],
 'Such a scaffold must not be used until a competent person has inspected it and provided written confirmation that it is complete.'),
('A scaffold is incomplete and left unattended overnight. What must be done?',
 'Tag and sign its access points', ['Leave a ladder against it', "Remove the bottom lift's planks", 'Tell the next shift by text'],
 'Unauthorised access to an incomplete scaffold must be prevented, for example with danger tags and warning signs at access points.'),
('When may you climb onto a mobile scaffold?',
 'Once its castors are locked', ['While it is being pushed', 'Once one wheel is chocked with timber', 'Before it is levelled'],
 'A mobile scaffold must not be accessed until its castors are locked, and it must never be moved while anyone is on it.'),
('How should you normally get onto a mobile scaffold platform?',
 'By its internal ladder', ['By climbing the outside frame', 'By standing on the guardrail', 'By a stepladder against it'],
 'Mobile scaffolds should be accessed by an internal ladder, often through a trapdoor, except for low platforms where that is not reasonably practicable.'),
('You need to remove a scaffold tie that is in your way. What should you do?',
 'Ask a scaffolder to alter it', ['Remove it and refit it later', 'Loosen it a little instead', 'Ask a co-worker to hold it'],
 'Workers must not make unauthorised alterations such as removing ties, guardrails, planks or toe-boards. Only competent, and where needed licensed, scaffolders alter a scaffold.'),
('In a boom-type EWP, where should your harness lanyard be attached?',
 'The designated anchor point', ['The nearest handrail', "The boom's hydraulic hose", 'The structure being worked on'],
 "Attach to the EWP's designated anchor point with a lanyard as short as possible. Use the handrail only if the manufacturer says it is the anchor point."),
('Unless it is built for rough terrain, what surface should an EWP be used on?',
 'A solid, level surface', ['Any firm, grassed area', 'A gentle, even slope', 'A gravel driveway'],
 'Most EWPs are designed for solid, level surfaces. Rough terrain models are the exception, and every set-up area should be checked for pits and obstructions.'),
('What is an EWP normally meant to be used as?',
 'A working platform', ['A crane for loads', 'A way onto the roof', 'A ladder support'],
 'An EWP is a work platform. It should not be used to enter or leave a work area at height unless the strict conditions in the relevant Australian Standard are met.'),
('Your pre-start check finds a damaged EWP control. What should you do?',
 'Tag it out and report it', ['Use it slowly and carefully', 'Fix it with tape and carry on', 'Mention it at the end of the day'],
 'Plant and equipment must be checked for serviceability before use, and faults rectified or reported. Tag out faulty equipment so no one else uses it.'),
('Trestle scaffolds are generally not suitable for working at what height?',
 '2 metres or above', ['1 metre or above', '3 metres or above', '4 metres or above'],
 'Trestle scaffolds are generally not suitable at 2 metres or above. If used higher, guardrails and toe-boards should be fitted to the open sides and ends.'),
("What should you use to set the height of a bricklayer's trestle?",
 'Its purpose-designed pins', ['Nails of the right length', 'Pieces of reinforcing bar', 'Any bolt that fits the hole'],
 'Only the purpose-designed pins should be used, ideally attached to the trestle by a chain. Nails and reo bar can bend or slip out.'),
('What point load should a cover over a floor penetration be able to withstand?',
 'At least 2 kN (about 200 kg)', ['At least 0.5 kN (about 50 kg)', 'At least 1 kN (about 100 kg)', 'At least 15 kN (about 1500 kg)'],
 'Covers over penetrations should safely withstand a point load of at least 2 kilonewtons, about 200 kg, and be fixed so they cannot be dislodged.'),
('Why is a loose sheet of plywood a poor cover for a floor hole?',
 'It looks like any other sheet', ['It is too heavy to lift off', 'It cannot be painted or marked', 'It is too strong to cut'],
 "A plywood cover can be mistaken for spare material, picked up and not replaced. Covers should be secured and signed, for example 'DANGER HOLE BENEATH'."),
('Which fall does roof safety mesh not prevent?',
 'A fall from the roof edge', ['A fall through the roof', 'A fall between the purlins', 'A fall onto the ceiling'],
 'Safety mesh is designed to stop internal falls through a roof. It does not protect the edge, so it is used with edge protection or other controls.'),
('Generally, how steep may the walking surface of a solid construction be?',
 'About 7 degrees (1 in 8)', ['About 2 degrees (1 in 30)', 'About 26 degrees (1 in 2)', 'About 35 degrees (1 in 1.4)'],
 'Surfaces of a solid construction should generally not exceed 7 degrees, a 1 in 8 gradient. Cleated surfaces may go to 20 degrees, a 1 in 3 gradient.'),
('When may a worker be raised by a forklift to work at height?',
 'Only in a compliant work platform', ['Standing on a pallet on the tynes', 'Standing on the tynes if held', 'On a ladder on the tynes'],
 'People may only be raised in a purpose-designed work platform secured to the forklift. Standing on tynes, pallets or ladders on a forklift is unacceptable.'),
('What should never be directly below a crane-suspended workbox?',
 'People', ['The crane', 'Its tag lines', 'The work area'],
 'A workbox or work platform must not be suspended over people. Set up an exclusion zone below the lift.'),
('A barrier at the edge of a mezzanine floor must be built to withstand what?',
 'A person falling against it', ['Wind loads from storms only', 'The weight of the stairs', 'A forklift reversing into it'],
 'Barriers at edges must be designed and constructed to withstand the force of someone falling against them. Guardrails, balustrades or mesh panels are typical.'),
('A trench is being dug where people walk past. Besides guardrails, what control may be used?',
 'A barrier set back about 1.5 m', ['A sign at the site gate only', 'A rope laid along the ground', 'A cone at each end of the trench'],
 'Trench fall risks can be controlled by guardrails, including on trench shields, or by a barrier set about 1.5 metres back from the excavation.'),
('A hoist landing on a mezzanine floor needs an opening in the guardrail. How should it be protected?',
 'With a gate or similar barrier', ['With a painted line on the floor', 'With a warning sign only', 'Left open during the shift'],
 'Where access is needed through edge protection, for example to a hoist, a gate or other means should stop a person falling through the gap.'),
]
